; replhl.ax - syntax highlighting and paren matching for a line being
; typed at the REPL prompt.
;
; PURE. Every function here is a function of its arguments: a String
; in, a painted String or a byte offset out. Nothing reads a terminal,
; nothing writes one, nothing keeps state between keystrokes. That is
; what lets the editor call `replHlPaint` on every key without a
; redraw budget, and what lets the whole subsystem be gated by
; `tests/selfhost/272-highlight.ax` with no pty anywhere.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE LEXER IS THE COMPILER'S OWN, AND IT RUNS ON A HALF-TYPED LINE.
;
; A REPL that tokenises with a second, ad-hoc scanner is a REPL that
; lies: it shows a string as unterminated where `check` sees it closed,
; or paints a name as a keyword the parser does not know. So this
; module calls `lex` (lexer.ax:888) and nothing else.
;
; The question that had to be answered before any of this was written
; is whether `lex` SURVIVES a partial, unbalanced, mid-edit buffer.
; Measured, not assumed - by running it over every prefix of
; `(println "hel` and printing the token stream:
;
;   [(println "]    LPAREN@0..1 IDENT@1..8 ERROR@9..10  EOF@10..10
;   [(println "h]   LPAREN@0..1 IDENT@1..8 ERROR@9..11  EOF@11..11
;   [(println "hel] LPAREN@0..1 IDENT@1..8 ERROR@9..13  EOF@13..13
;   [(println "hel"] LPAREN@0..1 IDENT@1..8 STRING@9..14 EOF@14..14
;
; It survives, and it is total by construction rather than by luck:
;
;   * `lexTokens` (lexer.ax:502) is a `while (< p len)` loop with no
;     early exit and no error return; every arm of `stepToken`
;     advances at least one byte and then it emits TK_EOF.
;   * THE LEXER RAISES NO DIAGNOSTIC AT ALL. A malformed construct
;     becomes a TK_ERROR or TK_ERROR_ESC token and lexing resumes;
;     turning one into a message is `lexErrDiag`'s job in parser.ax,
;     and only main.ax, lsp.ax and codegen.ax call it. Painting a
;     token stream therefore cannot produce a diagnostic.
;   * Bracket balance is never checked lexically, so an unclosed `(`
;     is not an error to the lexer - it is one LPAREN token.
;   * An unterminated block comment "runs to the end of the source and
;     is NOT an error" (skipBlockComment's own header).
;   * `replParenDepth` (repl.ax:1406) already does this in shipped
;     code: it calls `(lex src)` on the joined, unbalanced
;     continuation buffer for every physical line of a multi-line
;     entry.
;
; So there is no pending-remainder machinery here and no second
; tokenizer. Mid-edit incompleteness arrives as a TK_ERROR token whose
; span ends at the buffer end, and `hlClassOf` paints exactly that as
; HL_PEND - an unclosed literal shown as a literal in progress, not in
; red.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT THE MEASUREMENT ALSO SHOWED, AND WHAT IT COST.
;
; The gaps between consecutive token spans are NOT always whitespace
; and comments. Comments emit no token, so a gap normally holds only
; trivia - but a bad escape inside a string emits ONE TK_ERROR_ESC
; covering the two escape characters and nothing else, and lexing
; resumes past the whole literal. Printed gaps, measured:
;
;   ["a\q"]        GAP<"a> GAP<">
;   [(f '\q' 2)]   GAP< '> GAP<' >
;
; `"a` and `'` are program text sitting in a gap. A gap walker that
; assumed trivia would either drop those bytes (breaking the
; visLen invariant the editor's wrap logic rests on) or run a comment
; scan off the end of the buffer. `hlGap` therefore has a residue arm:
; anything that is not a `;` line comment or a `#|` block comment is
; HL_PLAIN, and both comment scans are bounded by the GAP END rather
; than by the buffer length, so a `#|` inside the wreckage of a broken
; literal cannot swallow the rest of the line.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; COLOURS COME FROM style.ax, NINE CONSTANTS, NO TENTH.
;
; ESC cannot be written as a string literal in Axiom at all -
; `isEscapeChar` accepts exactly \n \t \r \\ \" \' \0 - so `paint`
; synthesises byte 27 at run time and a colour spelled inline here
; would not compile. The role each constant already plays in the
; diagnostic renderer is what picks it:
;
;   keyword    SGR_SEC   the renderer's SECONDARY label colour
;                        (render.ax:776), the structural annotation
;   ident      unpainted highlights.scm's last rule is the catch-all
;                        `(identifier) @variable`; unpainted is the
;                        cheapest correct answer and keeps the common
;                        case free of seven wrapper bytes
;   number     SGR_FIX   the colour of a fix's literal replacement
;                        text (render.ax:687)
;   literal    SGR_CODE  main.ax:1697 paints the literal code token
;                        `AXnnnn` with it - the palette's "this is a
;                        literal" colour
;   comment    SGR_GUT   render.ax paints every piece of non-content
;                        furniture with it: the `|` gutter, `-->`,
;                        `...`, `=`. A comment is furniture.
;   punct      unpainted brackets that are not the cursor's pair must
;                        stay quiet or every line is a christmas tree
;   error      SGR_ERROR `sgrForSev 0`
;   match      SGR_HELP  the affirmative `help:` label's colour; a
;                        partner FOUND is the affirmative answer
;   mismatch   SGR_ERROR the same red, because it is the same claim
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE INVARIANT THE EDITOR DEPENDS ON.
;
;   (visLen (replHlPaint src cursor 0)) == (visLen src)
;
; `visLen` (style.ax:164) counts no escape sequence and no UTF-8
; continuation byte, so it is the display width. The spans this module
; produces cover every byte of the buffer exactly once, in order, and
; every escape goes around a WHOLE span - never around an individual
; character, which is style.ax's stated rule and what keeps the
; renderer's output diffable. Together those make the equality hold,
; and the fixture asserts it per case rather than trusting the
; argument. It is `visLen src` and NOT `strLen src`: `strLen` counts
; bytes, and a line holding `'e'` with an accent is more bytes than
; columns.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; PAREN MATCHING IS PAINT ONLY. `replParenDepth` IS NOT TOUCHED.
;
; repl.ax:1406 sums LPAREN/LBRACE/LBRACKET as +1 and the three closers
; as -1 into ONE counter with no floor, so `( ]` measures 0 and
; dispatches and depth can go negative. That answer decides whether
; the REPL waits for another line, which makes it PIPED-SURFACE
; behaviour: changing it would move byte goldens. So the matcher here
; is a separate typed walk with a stack, `( ]` is a mismatch on both
; brackets rather than a balance of zero - and `replHlDepth` recovers
; the plain counter from the same walk, so a gate can assert the two
; agree without either drifting into the other's job.
(import Mem)

(import Str)

(import Vec)

(import core)

(import lexer)

(import parser)

(import format)

(import style)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Classes. Plain Int constants in style.ax's shape rather than a
; `data`: a nullary constructor would need `cast Int` to compare, and
; MEMORY's rule for this tree is typed accessors only.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: HL_PLAIN Int)

(pub fn (HL_PLAIN) 0)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: HL_KEYWORD Int)

(pub fn (HL_KEYWORD) 1)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: HL_IDENT Int)

(pub fn (HL_IDENT) 2)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: HL_NUM Int)

(pub fn (HL_NUM) 3)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: HL_LIT Int)

(pub fn (HL_LIT) 4)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: HL_COMMENT Int)

(pub fn (HL_COMMENT) 5)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: HL_PUNCT Int)

(pub fn (HL_PUNCT) 6)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: HL_ERROR Int)

(pub fn (HL_ERROR) 7)

; An incomplete literal, painted AS a literal. See `hlClassOf`.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: HL_PEND Int)

(pub fn (HL_PEND) 8)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: HL_MATCH Int)

(pub fn (HL_MATCH) 9)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: HL_MISMATCH Int)

(pub fn (HL_MISMATCH) 10)

; One ASCII letter per class, for `replHlClass`. Pure ASCII and no
; escapes, so the whole classification is a string a fixture can pin
; by hand beside the source it describes.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: hlLetter (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (hlLetter cls)
  (if (== cls HL_KEYWORD)
    75
    (if (== cls HL_IDENT)
      105
      (if (== cls HL_NUM)
        110
        (if (== cls HL_LIT)
          115
          (if (== cls HL_COMMENT)
            99
            (if (== cls HL_PUNCT)
              112
              (if (== cls HL_ERROR)
                69
                (if (== cls HL_PEND)
                  63
                  (if (== cls HL_MATCH)
                    77
                    (if (== cls HL_MISMATCH)
                      88
                      46
                    )
                  )
                )
              )
            )
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

; The SGR parameter string a class is painted with, or "" for a class
; that is left unpainted. Built ONLY from style.ax's nine exported
; constants - a tenth colour has to be added there, beside the others,
; because ESC has no literal spelling in this language.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: hlSgr (-> Int String))

(pub fn (hlSgr cls)
  (if (== cls HL_KEYWORD)
    SGR_SEC
    (if (== cls HL_NUM)
      SGR_FIX
      (if (|| (== cls HL_LIT) (== cls HL_PEND))
        SGR_CODE
        (if (== cls HL_COMMENT)
          SGR_GUT
          (if (== cls HL_ERROR)
            SGR_ERROR
            (if (== cls HL_MATCH)
              SGR_HELP
              (if (== cls HL_MISMATCH)
                SGR_ERROR
                ""
              )
            )
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A classified byte range, half-open, mirroring core.ax's Span so the
; two read alike.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub struct HlSpan
  (cls : Int)
  (start : Int)
  (end : Int))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: mkHlSpan (-> Int Int Int Int))

(pub fn (mkHlSpan cls start end) (HlSpan cls start end))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: hlSpanCls (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (hlSpanCls s) (memGetWord s 0))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: hlSpanStart (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (hlSpanStart s) (memGetWord s 1))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: hlSpanEnd (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (hlSpanEnd s) (memGetWord s 2))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A bracket, its family, its partner's byte offset (-1 when it has
; none) and the index of its span - which is what lets `hlMarkPair`
; recolour the pair without a second search.
;
; `fam` is 0 for `()`, 1 for `{}`, 2 for `[]`. `open` is 1 or 0.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub struct HlBrk
  (pos : Int)
  (open : Int)
  (fam : Int)
  (partner : Int)
  (span : Int))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: mkHlBrk (-> Int Int Int Int Int Int))

(pub fn (mkHlBrk pos open fam partner span) (HlBrk pos open fam partner span))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: hlBrkPos (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (hlBrkPos b) (memGetWord b 0))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: hlBrkOpen (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (hlBrkOpen b) (memGetWord b 1))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: hlBrkFam (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (hlBrkFam b) (memGetWord b 2))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: hlBrkPartner (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (hlBrkPartner b) (memGetWord b 3))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: hlBrkSpan (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (hlBrkSpan b) (memGetWord b 4))

; The result of one lex: the classified spans, and the brackets.
; Returned together because they come from ONE `lex` call - painting a
; line and matching its brackets are two readings of the same token
; stream, and lexing twice per keystroke to keep two entry points
; tidy is work the editor pays for on every key.
(pub struct HlScan
  (spans : (Vec Int))
  (brks : (Vec Int)))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: mkHlScan (-> (Vec Int) (Vec Int) Int))

(pub fn (mkHlScan spans brks) (HlScan spans brks))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: hlScanSpans (-> Int (Vec Int)))

(pub fn (hlScanSpans s) (memGetWordVec s 0))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: hlScanBrks (-> Int (Vec Int)))

(pub fn (hlScanBrks s) (memGetWordVec s 1))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The twelve declaration heads.
;
; A COPY, deliberately and with the drift gated. `isDeclLine`
; (repl.ax:221) holds the same twelve inline inside one boolean over a
; whole LINE, so it is not callable as a name predicate, and this
; subsystem must not modify repl.ax. `scripts/check-repl-highlight.sh`
; extracts both lists and requires them equal, which is what turns a
; copy into a checked copy - the `impl`-was-missing comment sitting
; right below `isDeclLine` records what an unchecked one costs.
;
; format.ax's `isKeywordHead` covers the other seventeen (and four of
; these twelve); neither list alone is the keyword set.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: hlIsDeclHead (-> String Bool))

(pub fn (hlIsDeclHead w)
  (|| (strEq w "fn") (|| (strEq w "define") (|| (strEq w "::") (|| (strEq w "pub") (|| (strEq w "data") (|| (strEq w "struct") (|| (strEq w "import") (|| (strEq w "macro") (|| (strEq w "effect") (|| (strEq w "type") (|| (strEq w "trait") (strEq w "impl"))))))))))))
)

; A keyword is a POSITION, not a token kind, and this predicate only
; ever runs on a name in head position - see `hlClassOf`.
;
; format.ax:2391 records why that guard is load-bearing:
; `fpIsReservedWord`, thirty-two words reserved in EVERY position, was
; deleted on 2026-08-28 because it made `fmt` refuse `(fn (g data)
; data)` while `check` accepted it. A highlighter that paints `data`
; as a keyword there repeats the same defect in a new place, so the
; fixture carries that exact form as a named case.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: hlIsKeyword (-> String Bool))

(pub fn (hlIsKeyword w) (|| (isKeywordHead w) (hlIsDeclHead w)))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Push a span, dropping the empty ones. A zero-width span would paint
; nothing and would give `replHlClass` a letter to write nowhere.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(:: hlPush (-> (Vec Int) Int Int Int (Vec Int)))

(fn (hlPush out cls s e)
  (if (< s e)
    (vecPush
      out      (mkHlSpan cls s e)
    )
    out
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The trivia between two token spans: whitespace, comments, and - as
; the measurement in this file's header shows - the wreckage either
; side of a bad escape.
;
; The three scans are the LEXER'S OWN (`skipLineComment`,
; `isBlockOpen`, `skipBlockComment`), so nested `#| a #| b |# c |#` is
; one comment here exactly as it is one comment to the compiler, and a
; `;` inside a string never reaches this function because it lives
; inside a STRING token rather than in a gap.
;
; BOTH COMMENT SCANS ARE BOUNDED BY `b`, THE GAP END, not by the
; buffer length. Inside a gap that exists only because a literal was
; malformed, a stray `#|` would otherwise consume every remaining
; span and blow total coverage apart.
;
; The newline that ends a line comment stays HL_PLAIN: the editor
; splits the painted buffer on newlines, and a newline wrapped in a
; colour escape would put the reset on the next row.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(:: hlGap (-> String Int Int (Vec Int) (Vec Int)))

(fn (hlGap src a b out)
  (let ((mut q a))
    {
      (while (< q b)
        (let ((c (strByte src q)))
          (if (== c 59)
            (let ((r (skipLineComment src (+ q 1) b)))
              (let ((e (if (&& (> r q) (== (strByte src (- r 1)) 10))
                (- r 1)
                r
              )))
                {
                  (hlPush out HL_COMMENT q e)
                  (set q (if (> e q)
                    e
                    (+ q 1)
                  ))
                }
              )
            )
            (if (isBlockOpen src q b)
              (let ((r (skipBlockComment src (+ q 2) b)))
                {
                  (hlPush out HL_COMMENT q r)
                  (set q (if (> r q)
                    r
                    (+ q 1)
                  ))
                }
              )
              ; Residue: whitespace, or program text stranded by a bad
              ; escape. One span per run rather than one per byte.
              (let ((mut r (+ q 1)))
                {
                  (while (&& (< r b) (&& (!= (strByte src r) 59) (if (isBlockOpen src r b)
                      false
                      true
                    )))
                    (set r (+ r 1)))
                  (hlPush out HL_PLAIN q r)
                  (set q r)
                }
              )
            )
          )
        ))
      out
    }
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; One token to one class.
;
; `isHead` is "the previously emitted token was TK_LPAREN", which is
; the whole of the keyword rule; `n` is the buffer length, which is
; the whole of the pending rule.
;
; THE PENDING RULE, both halves of it. An unterminated string emits
; one TK_ERROR from the opening quote to the END OF SOURCE (lexer.ax's
; string arm: "since a newline no longer stops it"), and an
; unterminated char literal clamps its span to `len`. So a TK_ERROR
; ending exactly at the buffer end is what a literal in progress looks
; like - but so is a too-big integer literal, which also ends there
; and which no further digit can ever fix. The first byte separates
; them, and it separates them by the SAME dispatch `lexErrDiag` uses
; (only `"` can open a string, only `'` a char, only a digit an
; out-of-range number). A digit-led TK_ERROR therefore paints red at
; once, and `(println "hel` paints as a string in progress.
;
; TK_ERROR_ESC is always HL_ERROR: `\q` is settled, no further
; keystroke makes it an escape.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(:: hlClassOf (-> Int Bool Int Int))

(fn (hlClassOf t isHead n)
  (let (
    (k (kindInt (tokenKind t)))
    (lx (tokenLexeme t))
  )
    (if (|| (== k (kindInt TK_LPAREN)) (|| (== k (kindInt TK_RPAREN)) (|| (== k (kindInt TK_LBRACE)) (|| (== k (kindInt TK_RBRACE)) (|| (== k (kindInt TK_LBRACKET)) (|| (== k (kindInt TK_RBRACKET)) (|| (== k (kindInt TK_COLON)) (|| (== k (kindInt TK_COMMA)) (|| (== k (kindInt TK_DOT)) (|| (== k (kindInt TK_QUOTE)) (== k (kindInt TK_RARROW))))))))))))
      HL_PUNCT
      (if (== k (kindInt TK_DOUBLE_COLON))
        (if isHead
          HL_KEYWORD
          HL_PUNCT
        )
        (if (|| (== k (kindInt TK_INT)) (== k (kindInt TK_FLOAT)))
          HL_NUM
          (if (|| (== k (kindInt TK_STRING)) (== k (kindInt TK_CHARLIT)))
            HL_LIT
            (if (== k (kindInt TK_IDENT))
              (if (&& isHead (hlIsKeyword lx))
                HL_KEYWORD
                HL_IDENT
              )
              (if (== k (kindInt TK_ERROR))
                (let ((b0 (if (> (strLen lx) 0)
                  (strByte lx 0)
                  0
                )))
                  (if (&& (== (spanEnd (tokenSpan t)) n) (|| (== b0 34) (== b0 39)))
                    HL_PEND
                    HL_ERROR
                  )
                )
                (if (== k (kindInt TK_ERROR_ESC))
                  HL_ERROR
                  HL_PLAIN
                )
              )
            )
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

; The bracket family of a token kind: 0 `()`, 1 `{}`, 2 `[]`, -1 not a
; bracket.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: hlFamOf (-> Int Int))

(fn (hlFamOf k)
  (if (|| (== k (kindInt TK_LPAREN)) (== k (kindInt TK_RPAREN)))
    0
    (if (|| (== k (kindInt TK_LBRACE)) (== k (kindInt TK_RBRACE)))
      1
      (if (|| (== k (kindInt TK_LBRACKET)) (== k (kindInt TK_RBRACKET)))
        2
        (- 0 1)
      )
    )
  )
)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: hlIsOpener (-> Int Bool))

(fn (hlIsOpener k) (|| (== k (kindInt TK_LPAREN)) (|| (== k (kindInt TK_LBRACE)) (== k (kindInt TK_LBRACKET)))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The classifier. ONE `lex`, then every byte of the buffer covered
; exactly once and in order by an HlSpan, and every bracket token
; recorded with its span index.
;
; TK_EOF is skipped: its span is zero-width, and `lexTokens`'s own
; comment warns `p` may sit past `len` when the last token overshot.
; Every offset is clamped to [0, n] and forced forward, so a token
; that ever did overshoot cannot make coverage non-total - the
; property the visLen invariant rests on.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: hlScan (-> String Int))

(pub fn (hlScan src)
  (let (
    (toks (lex src))
    (n (strLen src))
    (spans vecNew)
    (brks vecNew)
    (mut i 0)
    (mut p 0)
    (mut prevLP false)
  )
    {
      (while (< i (vecLen toks))
        {
          (let ((t (vecGet toks i)))
            (let ((k (kindInt (tokenKind t))))
              (if (== k (kindInt TK_EOF))
                0
                (let (
                  (s0 (spanStart (tokenSpan t)))
                  (e0 (spanEnd (tokenSpan t)))
                )
                  (let ((s (if (< s0 p)
                    p
                    (if (> s0 n)
                      n
                      s0
                    )
                  )))
                    (let ((e (if (> e0 n)
                      n
                      (if (< e0 s)
                        s
                        e0
                      )
                    )))
                      {
                        (if (> s p)
                          (hlGap src p s spans)
                          spans
                        )
                        (if (>= (hlFamOf k) 0)
                          (vecPush
                            brks                            (mkHlBrk
                              s                              (if (hlIsOpener k)
                                1
                                0
                              )                              (hlFamOf k)                              (- 0 1)                              (vecLen spans)
                            )
                          )
                          brks
                        )
                        (hlPush spans (hlClassOf t prevLP n) s e)
                        (set prevLP (== k (kindInt TK_LPAREN)))
                        (set p e)
                      }
                    )
                  )
                )
              )
            )
          )
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      (if (< p n)
        (hlGap src p n spans)
        spans
      )
      (hlPair src spans brks)
      (mkHlScan spans brks)
    }
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Fill in every bracket's `partner`, with a typed stack.
;
; A Vec IS the stack - `vecPush`, `vecPop` and `vecLast` all exist, so
; no hand-rolled one is needed. It holds INDICES into `brks`, so
; writing a partner back is a `vecSet` on a rebuilt HlBrk.
;
; A CLOSER OF THE WRONG FAMILY POPS ANYWAY and leaves both brackets
; unpartnered. `( ]` is then a mismatch on the `(` and on the `]`,
; which is the answer a person typing wants and is exactly what
; `replParenDepth`'s single mixed counter cannot say - it measures 0
; and dispatches. Popping rather than leaving the opener on the stack
; is what stops one wrong closer from cascading: every later bracket
; in the line would otherwise be reported wrong too.
;
; Leftover openers keep partner -1. That is the ordinary mid-edit
; state, not an error: `(println "hel` has an unclosed `(` at every
; keystroke.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(:: hlSetPartner (-> (Vec Int) Int Int (Vec Int)))

(fn (hlSetPartner brks i partner)
  (let ((b (vecGet brks i)))
    (vecSet
      brks      i      (mkHlBrk
        (hlBrkPos b)        (hlBrkOpen b)        (hlBrkFam b)        partner        (hlBrkSpan b)
      )
    )
  )
)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(:: hlPair (-> String (Vec Int) (Vec Int) (Vec Int)))

(fn (hlPair src spans brks)
  (let (
    (n (vecLen brks))
    (stack vecNew)
    (mut i 0)
  )
    {
      (while (< i n)
        {
          (let ((b (vecGet brks i)))
            (if (== (hlBrkOpen b) 1)
              (vecPush
                stack                i
              )
              (if (== (vecLen stack) 0)
                brks
                (let ((j (vecPop stack)))
                  (let ((o (vecGet brks j)))
                    (if (== (hlBrkFam o) (hlBrkFam b))
                      {
                        (hlSetPartner brks j (hlBrkPos b))
                        (hlSetPartner brks i (hlBrkPos o))
                      }
                      brks
                    )
                  )
                )
              )
            )
          )
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      brks
    }
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The bracket the cursor is ON, as an index into `brks`, or -1.
;
; PREFER THE BRACKET ENDING AT THE CURSOR over the one starting at it.
; In a REPL the cursor sits just after the character you typed, so the
; delimiter you want to see acknowledged is the `)` you just closed,
; not the one you are about to step onto.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: hlBrkAt (-> (Vec Int) Int Int))

(pub fn (hlBrkAt brks cursor)
  (let (
    (n (vecLen brks))
    (mut i 0)
    (mut before (- 0 1))
    (mut on (- 0 1))
  )
    {
      (while (< i n)
        {
          (let ((p (hlBrkPos (vecGet brks i))))
            {
              (set before (if (== p (- cursor 1))
                i
                before
              ))
              (set on (if (== p cursor)
                i
                on
              ))
            }
          )
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      (if (>= before 0)
        before
        on
      )
    }
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Recolour the cursor's bracket and its partner in place.
;
; HL_MATCH on both when a partner exists, HL_MISMATCH on the bracket
; alone when it does not. `vecSet` overwrites the class and nothing
; else, so coverage stays total and the visLen invariant survives the
; marking - which is why the fixture measures visLen with a cursor set
; as well as without.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: hlMarkPair (-> (Vec Int) (Vec Int) Int (Vec Int)))

(pub fn (hlMarkPair spans brks cursor)
  (let ((i (hlBrkAt brks cursor)))
    (if (< i 0)
      spans
      (let ((b (vecGet brks i)))
        (if (< (hlBrkPartner b) 0)
          (hlRecolour spans (hlBrkSpan b) HL_MISMATCH)
          (let ((j (hlBrkIndexAt brks (hlBrkPartner b))))
            {
              (hlRecolour spans (hlBrkSpan b) HL_MATCH)
              (if (>= j 0)
                (hlRecolour spans (hlBrkSpan (vecGet brks j)) HL_MATCH)
                spans
              )
            }
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: hlBrkIndexAt (-> (Vec Int) Int Int))

; The bracket AT exactly this byte offset, or -1. Direct rather than
; reusing `hlBrkAt`, whose cursor rule prefers the bracket that ENDS
; at its argument: correct there, needless indirection here.
(fn (hlBrkIndexAt brks pos)
  (let (
    (n (vecLen brks))
    (mut i 0)
    (mut got (- 0 1))
  )
    {
      (while (< i n)
        {
          (set got (if (== (hlBrkPos (vecGet brks i)) pos)
            i
            got
          ))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      got
    }
  )
)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(:: hlRecolour (-> (Vec Int) Int Int (Vec Int)))

(fn (hlRecolour spans i cls)
  (if (|| (< i 0) (>= i (vecLen spans)))
    spans
    (let ((s (vecGet spans i)))
      (vecSet
        spans        i        (mkHlSpan cls (hlSpanStart s) (hlSpanEnd s))
      )
    )
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; PUBLIC SURFACE. Four pure functions; the editor needs no others.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Source and cursor to one ASCII class letter per source BYTE.
;
; `.` plain  `K` keyword  `i` ident  `n` number  `s` literal
; `c` comment  `p` punct  `E` error  `?` literal in progress
; `M` matched bracket  `X` unmatched bracket
;
; This is the subsystem's testable surface: every letter is one
; classification decision a fixture can pin by hand, beside the source
; it describes.
;
; THE FILL IS `_`, WHICH IS NOT A CLASS LETTER. The string is one
; `strAlloc` of the source length, so comparing its LENGTH to the
; source's proves nothing - it is that length by construction, and a
; check that cannot fail is worse than no check. Filling with a byte
; no class can produce is what makes the string report coverage
; honestly instead: a `_` in the output is a source byte no span
; covered, which should be impossible, and it is the classifier saying
; so about itself rather than a caller having to re-derive it.
;
; A cursor of -1 means "no cursor", so no bracket is marked.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: replHlClass (-> String Int String))

(pub fn (replHlClass src cursor)
  (let (
    (sc (hlScan src))
    (n (strLen src))
  )
    (let (
      (spans (hlMarkPair (hlScanSpans sc) (hlScanBrks sc) cursor))
      (out (strAlloc n))
      (mut i 0)
    )
      {
        (while (< i n)
          {
            (memPutByte (strData out) i 95)
            (set i (+ i 1))
          })
        (set i 0)
        (while (< i (vecLen spans))
          {
            (let ((s (vecGet spans i)))
              (let (
                (b (hlLetter (hlSpanCls s)))
                (mut q (hlSpanStart s))
              )
                (while (< q (hlSpanEnd s))
                  {
                    (if (&& (>= q 0) (< q n))
                      (memPutByte (strData out) q b)
                      0
                    )
                    (set q (+ q 1))
                  })
              )
            )
            (set i (+ i 1))
          })
        out
      }
    )
  )
)

; Source, cursor and the byte offset the visible line starts at, to
; the painted bytes for [from, end).
;
; It lexes the WHOLE buffer and renders only the tail, because a `)`
; on the third physical line of a continuation can only match a `(` on
; the first - the same buffer `replMain` is already accumulating and
; already handing to `replParenDepth`. `from` of 0 paints everything.
;
; One fragment per span into a Vec, joined ONCE with `catStrs`.
; `strConcat` folded over the fragments would peak at the sum of its
; intermediates, which is the discipline style.ax:115-118 and
; diag.ax's `catStrs` header both record; painting happens on every
; keystroke, which is the worst place to pay it.
;
; Escapes go around WHOLE spans, never around individual characters -
; style.ax:39-45's stated rule, and the reason `visLen` can undo the
; painting exactly.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: replHlPaint (-> String Int Int String))

(pub fn (replHlPaint src cursor from)
  (let (
    (sc (hlScan src))
    (n (strLen src))
    (parts vecNew)
    (mut i 0)
  )
    (let ((spans (hlMarkPair (hlScanSpans sc) (hlScanBrks sc) cursor)))
      {
        (while (< i (vecLen spans))
          {
            (let ((s (vecGet spans i)))
              (let (
                (a (if (< (hlSpanStart s) from)
                  from
                  (hlSpanStart s)
                ))
                (b (hlSpanEnd s))
              )
                (if (< a b)
                  (let (
                    (txt (strSlice src a (- b a)))
                    (sgr (hlSgr (hlSpanCls s)))
                  )
                    (if (> (strLen sgr) 0)
                      (vecPush parts (paint sgr txt))
                      (vecPush parts txt)
                    )
                  )
                  parts
                )
              )
            )
            (set i (+ i 1))
          })
        (catStrs parts)
      }
    )
  )
)

; The whole buffer, painted, with no cursor - the plain entry point.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: replHlLine (-> String String))

(pub fn (replHlLine src) (replHlPaint src (- 0 1) 0))

; Source and cursor to the byte offset of the MATCHING delimiter.
;
;   >= 0  the partner's offset
;   -1    there is no delimiter at the cursor at all
;   -2    there is one, and it has no partner (unclosed, unopened, or
;         closed by the wrong family)
;
; Three answers rather than two because the editor does different
; things with them: -1 draws nothing, -2 is the red flash that says
; "this bracket is wrong", and an offset is the pair to light up. A
; single -1 for both would make "no bracket here" and "this bracket is
; broken" indistinguishable, which is the whole feedback.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: replHlMatch (-> String Int Int))

(pub fn (replHlMatch src cursor)
  (let ((brks (hlScanBrks (hlScan src))))
    (let ((i (hlBrkAt brks cursor)))
      (if (< i 0)
        (- 0 1)
        (let ((p (hlBrkPartner (vecGet brks i))))
          (if (< p 0)
            (- 0 2)
            p
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

; The plain +1/-1 bracket count, recovered from the typed walk.
;
; It exists so a gate can assert this module and `replParenDepth`
; (repl.ax:1406) agree on a corpus, WITHOUT either drifting into the
; other's job. `replParenDepth`'s answer decides whether the REPL
; waits for another line, which makes it piped-surface behaviour and
; not something a highlighter may change; this number is the
; reconciliation between the two walks.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: replHlDepth (-> String Int))

(pub fn (replHlDepth src)
  (let (
    (brks (hlScanBrks (hlScan src)))
    (mut i 0)
    (mut d 0)
  )
    {
      (while (< i (vecLen brks))
        {
          (set d (if (== (hlBrkOpen (vecGet brks i)) 1)
            (+ d 1)
            (- d 1)
          ))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      d
    }
  )
)
